{
  "id": "a1-12",
  "kind": "lesson",
  "level": "A1",
  "order": 15,
  "title": "Numerals, Time, and Dates",
  "summary": "The numbers to a thousand and the four things a learner counts with them: the hour, the day of the week, the date, and the year. ¶58 settled which case a numeral governs; here are the numerals themselves.",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "cardinals-one-nine",
      "number": "79",
      "heading": "The Cardinals One to Nine",
      "statement": "The first nine numerals are **adjectives in all but name**: they have gender, they decline, and they agree with what they count (¶58). *Vienas* has a singular; the rest are plural in form, since two of a thing are more than one. **Du** is irregular in the feminine — *dvi*, not *dva* — and must be learnt by itself.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "One to nine, masculine and feminine",
          "columns": [
            "figure",
            "masculine",
            "feminine"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "1",
              "cells": [
                "vienas",
                "viena"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "2",
              "cells": [
                "du",
                "dvi"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "3",
              "cells": [
                "trys",
                "trys"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "4",
              "cells": [
                "keturi",
                "keturios"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "5",
              "cells": [
                "penki",
                "penkios"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "6",
              "cells": [
                "šeši",
                "šešios"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "7",
              "cells": [
                "septyni",
                "septynios"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "8",
              "cells": [
                "aštuoni",
                "aštuonios"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "9",
              "cells": [
                "devyni",
                "devynios"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "*Trys* is the same in both genders, as *šis* is not; it is the only one that does not distinguish them."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Turiu du brolius ir dvi seseris.",
          "gloss": "I have two brothers and two sisters.",
          "note": "the numeral takes the gender of its noun and the case of the sentence"
        },
        {
          "target": "Čia yra keturios kėdės.",
          "gloss": "There are four chairs here."
        },
        {
          "target": "Reikia dviejų stiklinių vandens.",
          "gloss": "Two glasses of water are needed.",
          "note": "*reikia* puts everything after it in the genitive (¶69), the numeral included"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "numeral-case",
        "adjective-agreement",
        "reikia"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "cardinals-ten-up",
      "number": "80",
      "heading": "Ten and Upwards",
      "statement": "From ten the numerals stop agreeing and start governing: they stand unchanged and put the counted noun in the **genitive plural**, whatever the sentence would otherwise require (¶58). The tens are built on the units, the teens on the units with **-lika**, and *šimtas* and *tūkstantis* are ordinary nouns that happen to mean a hundred and a thousand.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Ten to a thousand",
          "columns": [
            "figure",
            "Lithuanian"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "10",
              "cells": [
                "dešimt"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "11–19",
              "cells": [
                "vienuolika, dvylika, trylika, keturiolika, penkiolika, šešiolika, septyniolika, aštuoniolika, devyniolika"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "20–50",
              "cells": [
                "dvidešimt, trisdešimt, keturiasdešimt, penkiasdešimt"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "60–90",
              "cells": [
                "šešiasdešimt, septyniasdešimt, aštuoniasdešimt, devyniasdešimt"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "100, 1000",
              "cells": [
                "šimtas, tūkstantis"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "In a compound number the **last word** decides the construction: *dvidešimt vienas namas* has a singular noun agreeing with *vienas*, while *dvidešimt du namai* has a plural agreeing with *du*, and *dvidešimt penki namai* likewise."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Penkiolika knygų.",
          "gloss": "Fifteen books.",
          "note": "genitive plural, fixed"
        },
        {
          "target": "Šimtas eurų.",
          "gloss": "A hundred euros."
        },
        {
          "target": "Dvidešimt vienas žmogus.",
          "gloss": "Twenty-one people.",
          "note": "singular, because *vienas* is the last word"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "numeral-case",
        "plural-genitive",
        "cardinals-one-nine"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ordinals",
      "number": "81",
      "heading": "The Ordinals",
      "statement": "The **ordinals** — first, second, third — are adjectives outright, declined and agreeing like *geras*. They are what Lithuanian uses for floors, for the hour, and for the day of the month, so they are met far oftener than their English equivalents.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "The first ten ordinals",
          "columns": [
            "figure",
            "masculine",
            "feminine"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "1st",
              "cells": [
                "pirmas",
                "pirma"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "2nd",
              "cells": [
                "antras",
                "antra"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "3rd",
              "cells": [
                "trečias",
                "trečia"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "4th",
              "cells": [
                "ketvirtas",
                "ketvirta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "5th",
              "cells": [
                "penktas",
                "penkta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "6th",
              "cells": [
                "šeštas",
                "šešta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "7th",
              "cells": [
                "septintas",
                "septinta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "8th",
              "cells": [
                "aštuntas",
                "aštunta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "9th",
              "cells": [
                "devintas",
                "devinta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "10th",
              "cells": [
                "dešimtas",
                "dešimta"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "Above ten they are formed straightforwardly: *vienuoliktas*, *dvyliktas*, *dvidešimtas*. In a compound only the last word is an ordinal: *dvidešimt pirmas*."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš gyvenu pirmame aukšte.",
          "gloss": "I live on the first floor.",
          "note": "locative, because the floor is a place"
        },
        {
          "target": "Tai antra knyga.",
          "gloss": "That is the second book."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
