        "locative-singular"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "telling-the-hour",
      "number": "82",
      "heading": "Telling the Hour",
      "statement": "The hour is a **feminine ordinal** agreeing with *valanda*, which is usually left out: *pirma* means one o'clock because *pirma valanda* does. The question is **kelinta valanda?**, literally *the how-manieth hour?* The half hour is *pusė* with the **genitive** of the hour coming, and the minutes before the hour are **be** with the genitive — the Lithuanian counts down to an hour it has not yet reached.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Saying the time",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "English",
            "literally"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "on the hour",
              "cells": [
                "Dabar pirma.",
                "It is one o'clock.",
                "now first"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "on the hour",
              "cells": [
                "Dabar dešimta valanda.",
                "It is ten o'clock.",
                "now tenth hour"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "half",
              "cells": [
                "Pusė dešimtos.",
                "Half past nine.",
                "half of the tenth"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "before the hour",
              "cells": [
                "Be penkiolikos dešimta.",
                "A quarter to ten.",
                "without fifteen, the tenth"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "at what hour",
              "cells": [
                "Pirmą valandą.",
                "At one o'clock.",
                "accusative of time (¶83)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "*Pusė dešimtos* is half past **nine**, not half past ten. The Lithuanian names the hour being filled, as the clock face does; the English names the one just past. Mistaking this costs an hour."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Kelinta valanda?",
          "gloss": "What time is it?"
        },
        {
          "target": "Dirbu nuo aštuntos iki penktos.",
          "gloss": "I work from eight until five.",
          "note": "*nuo* and *iki* both take the genitive (¶33)"
        },
        {
          "target": "Susitinkame antrą valandą.",
          "gloss": "We are meeting at two o'clock."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "ordinals",
        "genitive-prepositions",
        "question-adverbs"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "accusative-of-time",
      "number": "83",
      "heading": "The Accusative of Time",
      "statement": "A **point** of time and a **stretch** of time both stand in the accusative, with no preposition. This is the case that answers *kada?* for a single occasion — set it against the plural instrumental of ¶76, which answers the same question for a habit. *Pirmadienį* is on Monday, once; *pirmadieniais* is on Mondays, always.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Point and stretch",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "English",
            "use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "day",
              "cells": [
                "Susitinkame pirmadienį.",
                "We are meeting on Monday.",
                "point"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "season",
              "cells": [
                "Žiemą grįšiu.",
                "In winter I shall return.",
                "point"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "hour",
              "cells": [
                "Pirmą valandą.",
                "At one o'clock.",
                "point"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "duration",
              "cells": [
                "Londone buvau savaitę.",
                "I was in London for a week.",
                "stretch"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Vieną dieną dirbu, kitą ilsiuosi.",
          "gloss": "One day I work, the next I rest."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mes gyvenome Kaune metus.",
          "gloss": "We lived in Kaunas for a year.",
          "note": "duration; no *for* is needed"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "accusative-nouns",
        "instrumental-time-route",
        "question-adverbs"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "days-of-week",
      "number": "84",
      "heading": "The Days of the Week",
      "statement": "The names of the days are built transparently on the ordinals plus **diena**, day — the first day, the second day, and so on from Monday. Only Sunday breaks the pattern. They are masculine, they are written with a small letter, and *pirmadienis* being the **first** day is a reminder that the Lithuanian week begins on Monday.",
